{"parentUuid":"e3d78862-a4cc-4fac-9191-f840e957560f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q2m1QEqAhqdPfVWXcLyn4r","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DEAP pymoo PyGAD CP-SAT integration python performance benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Benchmarks — DEAP 1.4.3 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://deap.readthedocs.io/en/master/api/benchmarks.html\"},{\"title\":\"EvoX: A Distributed GPU-accelerated Framework for ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.12457\"},{\"title\":\"pymoo: Multi-objective Optimization in Python — pymoo: Multi-objective Optimization in Python 0.6.1.6 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pymoo.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarks — DEAP 1.4.1 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://deap.readthedocs.io/en/stable/api/benchmarks.html\"},{\"title\":\"Thefittest: evolutionary machine learning in Python\",\"url\":\"https://www.itm-conferences.org/articles/itmconf/pdf/2024/02/itmconf_hmmocs2023_02020.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - DEAP/deap: Distributed Evolutionary Algorithms in Python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/DEAP/deap\"},{\"title\":\"進化計算ライブラリ10選\",\"url\":\"https://tomtkg.github.io/bib.html\"},{\"title\":\"Performance Indicator — pymoo: Multi-objective Optimization in Python 0.6.1.6 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pymoo.org/misc/indicators.html\"},{\"title\":\"deap/deap/benchmarks/tools.py at master · DEAP/deap\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/DEAP/deap/blob/master/deap/benchmarks/tools.py\"},{\"title\":\"Genetic algorithm from scratch vs library. Which is one is better? | ResearchGate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/post/Genetic_algorithm_from_scratch_vs_library_Which_is_one_is_better\"}]\n\nI found search results related to DEAP, pymoo, and PyGAD - three popular Python frameworks for evolutionary algorithms and optimization. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Frameworks\n\nDEAP is a comprehensive framework for evolutionary algorithms in Python with a rich history and features catering to both single- and multi-objective variants. pymoo is an open source framework for multi-objective optimization in Python that provides state-of-the-art single- and multi-objective optimization algorithms along with visualization and decision-making features.\n\n## Performance Comparison\n\nA performance benchmark study compared execution times across these packages, with results showing different algorithms had varying speeds across DEAP, PyGAD, and Pymoo. The study found that differential evolution algorithms implemented with the Numba library achieved significantly faster execution times compared to other implementations.\n\n## Integration and Features\n\nPyGAD is tightly integrated with Keras and PyTorch, allowing GPU usage when training neural networks with genetic algorithms.\n\n**Note:** The search results did not contain specific information about CP-SAT integration with these frameworks. CP-SAT is Google's constraint solver, and integrating it with these evolutionary algorithm libraries would require more specialized documentation or research papers that weren't returned in these results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"2187bf8f-367f-42b8-837b-0ca6054aafec","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:50:47.207Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e3d78862-a4cc-4fac-9191-f840e957560f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e652d4fc-0148-4c29-b599-d5bef05658f6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015imd7XckT32n7XWPvGihwd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"neural guided genetic algorithm GNN crossover combinatorial optimization 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Solving Combinatorial Optimization Problems with Graph Neural Networks and Genetic Algorithms: Application to Road Networks[v1] | Preprints.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.preprints.org/manuscript/202512.0393\"},{\"title\":\"An alternative approach for neural network evolution with a genetic algorithm: crossover by combinatorial optimization - PubMed\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16343847/\"},{\"title\":\"An alternative approach for neural network evolution with a genetic algorithm: Crossover by combinatorial optimization - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0893608005002297\"},{\"title\":\"Learning to Explore and Exploit with GNNs for Unsupervised Combinatorial Optimization | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=vaJ4FObpXN\"},{\"title\":\"An alternative approach for neural network evolution with a genetic algorithm: Crossover by combinatorial optimization | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/7425930_An_alternative_approach_for_neural_network_evolution_with_a_genetic_algorithm_Crossover_by_combinatorial_optimization\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - dongjinkun/COPZoo: Neural Combinatorial Optimization · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/dongjinkun/COPZoo\"},{\"title\":\"Guiding Genetic Programming with Graph Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2411.05820v1\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2025\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/gomes/pdf/2025_acikalin_iclr_x2gnn.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Unsupervised learning with GNNs for QUBO-based combinatorial optimization - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2192440625000139\"},{\"title\":\"Primal-Dual Graph Neural Networks for General NP-Hard Combinatorial Optimization | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=4Hd7u3LHlZ\"}]\n\nI've found several relevant research papers and resources related to neural-guided genetic algorithms, GNNs, and combinatorial optimization from 2025. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Recent 2025 Research\n\nA hybrid method combining Graph Neural Networks with Genetic Algorithms proposes using GNNs to predict a closure potential score for each road (edge), and biases the GA's initial solution generation and mutation operations accordingly. This hybrid method reduced average travel time by approximately 3% compared to using GA alone.\n\n## GNN-Guided Combinatorial Optimization\n\nThere has been growing interest in combining deep learning–based heuristics with metaheuristic search for combinatorial optimization, with Graph Neural Networks demonstrating strong capability in learning structural patterns in graphs and being applied to problems such as the Traveling Salesman Problem, Vehicle Routing Problem, and network design.\n\nA novel unsupervised neural framework called X²GNN combines exploration and exploitation for combinatorial search optimization, with exploration generating multiple solutions simultaneously to promote diversity in the search space, and exploitation employing neural stochastic iterative refinement to guide search toward promising regions.\n\n## Additional 2025 Developments\n\nThe search results also show numerous 2025 papers in neural combinatorial optimization, including work on vehicle routing problems, traveling salesman problems, and various learning-guided heuristic approaches that combine neural networks with evolutionary and optimization techniques.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5ff1208f-bf7b-4be2-95e9-4e04d13b2b35","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:50:49.321Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e652d4fc-0148-4c29-b599-d5bef05658f6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"28ed3234-ed8d-4a8a-9390-400d828fa166","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMVWHDvViwzqbWKoSf7vPL","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"hyper-heuristic operator selection 2D packing differential evolution PSO comparison\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Self-Adaptive Differential Evolution Hyper-Heuristic with Applications in Process Design\",\"url\":\"https://www.scielo.org.mx/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S1405-55462016000200173\"},{\"title\":\"A hybrid differential evolution particle swarm optimization algorithm based on dynamic strategies | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-82648-5\"},{\"title\":\"Hyper-heuristic Bibliography Mustafa Mısır, Xuanang Zhou\",\"url\":\"https://mustafamisir.github.io/HH.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Particle Swarm Optimization or Differential Evolution—A comparison - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0952197623001926\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Evolutionary hyper-heuristics for tackling bi-objective 2D bin packing problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/315897992_Evolutionary_hyper-heuristics_for_tackling_bi-objective_2D_bin_packing_problems\"},{\"title\":\"Hyper-Heuristic Approach for Tuning Parameter Adaptation in Differential Evolution\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-1680/13/1/59\"},{\"title\":\"A hyper-heuristic approach based on adaptive selection operator and behavioral schema for global optimization | Soft Computing | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00500-023-09018-7\"},{\"title\":\"Robust hyper-heuristic algorithms for the offline oriented/non-oriented 2D bin packing problems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1568494615004561\"},{\"title\":\"Hyper-heuristic Operator Selection and Acceptance Criteria | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/294278098_Hyper-heuristic_Operator_Selection_and_Acceptance_Criteria\"},{\"title\":\"An Adaptive Hybrid Algorithm Based on Particle Swarm Optimization and Differential Evolution for Global Optimization - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3934314/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding hyper-heuristic operator selection in 2D packing problems with comparisons between differential evolution and PSO:\n\n## Key Research Areas\n\n**Hyper-heuristic Approaches for 2D Packing:**\n\nHyper-heuristics have been employed in several applications such as the bin packing problem, 2D strip packing, production scheduling, constraint satisfaction problems, and the vehicle routing problem.\n\nA multi-objective evolutionary framework to build selection hyper-heuristics for solving instances of the 2D bin packing problem uses a multi-objective evolutionary learning process with specific tailored genetic operators to produce sets of variable length rules representing hyper-heuristics, with each hyper-heuristic building a solution by sensing the state of the instance and deciding which single heuristic to apply at each decision point.\n\n**Operator Selection in Hyper-heuristics:**\n\nCertain mutation operators are more oriented toward exploitation, e.g., DE/best/1, whereas others are more oriented toward exploration, e.g., DE/rand/1, making it difficult to choose the most efficient mutation operator.\n\n**DE vs PSO Comparisons:**\n\nEach move of DE algorithms is mainly a function of the current location of DE solutions, accepting new positions only if better than the previous one, while in PSO, particles move to the new location regardless of performance but remember the best location visited so far and are governed by current location, best historical position, recent move size, and best locations visited by other individuals in the swarm.\n\nA hybrid PSO-DE algorithm (HPSO-DE) enjoys the advantages of PSO and DE while maintaining population diversity, with competitive performance compared with PSO, DE, and their variants.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1d023ca8-cb9e-4e1e-b070-64cfa59574e9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:50:49.693Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"28ed3234-ed8d-4a8a-9390-400d828fa166","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0d1cfb90-eec5-43de-9094-adedd0f1680b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rf9SjHXW89StwRaHhp82iu","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"memetic algorithm vs LNS large neighborhood search comparison combinatorial\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Adaptive Large Neighborhood Search - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/adaptive-large-neighborhood-search\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Large Neighborhood Search Ravichandra Addanki MIT addanki@mit.edu\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=xEQhKANoVW\"},{\"title\":\"[2107.10201] Learning a Large Neighborhood Search Algorithm for Mixed Integer Programs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.10201\"},{\"title\":\"How to Choose Solutions for Local Search in Multiobjective Combinatorial Memetic Algorithms | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-15844-5_52\"},{\"title\":\"A General Large Neighborhood Search Framework for ...\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/file/e769e03a9d329b2e864b4bf4ff54ff39-Paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | A machine learning framework for neighbor generation in metaheuristic search\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/applied-mathematics-and-statistics/articles/10.3389/fams.2023.1128181/full\"},{\"title\":\"Memetic Algorithm for Intense Local Search Methods Using Local Search Chains | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-88439-2_5\"},{\"title\":\"Evolutionary Computation in Combinatorial Optimization: 17th European Conference, EvoCOP 2017, Amsterdam, The Netherlands, April 19-21, 2017, Proceedings | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/321530449_Evolutionary_Computation_in_Combinatorial_Optimization_17th_European_Conference_EvoCOP_2017_Amsterdam_The_Netherlands_April_19-21_2017_Proceedings\"},{\"title\":\"Memetic algorithm - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memetic_algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"Large Neighborhood Search (LNS)\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/algorithms/large-neighborhood-search-lns\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comparison of memetic algorithms and Large Neighborhood Search (LNS) for combinatorial optimization:\n\n## Key Differences\n\n**Memetic Algorithms (MA):**\nA memetic algorithm is an extension of an evolutionary algorithm that aims to accelerate evolutionary search and uses one or more suitable heuristics or local search techniques to improve solution quality and speed up the search. Memetic algorithms represent a marriage between a population-based global search and heuristic local search made by each individual.\n\n**Large Neighborhood Search (LNS):**\nLNS is a heuristic optimization method designed to improve solutions to complex combinatorial and Mixed-Integer problems. It extends local search by exploring much larger portions of the solution space at each iteration. Instead of applying small modifications to a solution, LNS partially destroys a solution and then rebuilds it by solving a subproblem.\n\n## Hybrid Integration\n\nInterestingly, these approaches can be combined rather than used as alternatives. The credit assignment strategy for choosing different destroy-and-repair methods has been used at the local improvement stage of a memetic algorithm, which demonstrates good results with regards to solution quality and search time. Additionally, an NSGA-II-based memetic algorithm has been combined with adaptive large neighborhood search for integrated scheduling problems.\n\n## Problem Domain\n\nLNS is widely used in applications such as vehicle routing, scheduling, resource allocation, and other large-scale combinatorial optimization problems. Both approaches are effective metaheuristics for combinatorial optimization, but they work through different mechanisms—MAs use population-based evolution with local refinement, while LNS focuses on neighborhood exploration through destroy-repair cycles.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8ca5f512-87cf-4ad5-894e-efd40e13d5f0","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:50:50.123Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0d1cfb90-eec5-43de-9094-adedd0f1680b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
