{"parentUuid":"d11069b3-0b2b-4590-9f13-f32d92993e71","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019A8aHJyaggBwAbx5yT2WyA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"biased random key genetic algorithm BRKGA 2D packing benchmark gap optimal\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SciELO Brazil - A BIASED RANDOM-KEY GENETIC ALGORITHM FOR THE 2D STRIP PACKING PROBLEM WITH ORDER AND STABILITY CONSTRAINTS A BIASED RANDOM-KEY GENETIC ALGORITHM FOR THE 2D STRIP PACKING PROBLEM WITH ORDER AND STABILITY CONSTRAINTS\",\"url\":\"https://www.scielo.br/j/pope/a/6rm6JsNbRnLQJrr8YJgbFzk/?lang=en\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - dasvision0212/3D-Bin-Packing-Problem-with-BRKGA: An implementation ofr Biased Random Key Genetic Algorithmn for 3D Bin Packing Problem.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/dasvision0212/3D-Bin-Packing-Problem-with-BRKGA\"},{\"title\":\"Packing with biased random-key genetic algorithms topic of EBC Speaker Series | Penn State Berks\",\"url\":\"https://berks.psu.edu/story/2214/2014/09/29/packing-biased-random-key-genetic-algorithms-topic-ebc-speaker-series\"},{\"title\":\"Biased random-key genetic algorithms: A review - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0377221724002303\"},{\"title\":\"A biased random key genetic algorithm for 2D and 3D bin packing problems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0925527313001837\"},{\"title\":\"A biased random key genetic algorithm for 2D and 3D bin packing problems\",\"url\":\"https://scite.ai/reports/a-biased-random-key-genetic-61LAek\"},{\"title\":\"A parallel multi-population biased random-key genetic algorithm for a container loading problem - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054811000827\"},{\"title\":\"A biased random key genetic algorithm for 2D and 3D bin packing problems | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277440025_A_biased_random_key_genetic_algorithm_for_2D_and_3D_bin_packing_problems\"},{\"title\":\"Bin Packing Optimization via Deep Reinforcement Learning\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.12420\"},{\"title\":\"Biased Ranom-Key Genetic Algorithms: An Advanced Tutorial | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/305727818_Biased_Ranom-Key_Genetic_Algorithms_An_Advanced_Tutorial\"}]\n\nI've found comprehensive information about biased random-key genetic algorithms (BRKGA) applied to 2D packing problems. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview\n\nA biased random-key genetic algorithm (BRKGA) is a general search metaheuristic for finding optimal or near-optimal solutions to hard combinatorial optimization problems, derived from the random-key genetic algorithm of Bean (1994).\n\n## Application to 2D Packing\n\nNovel BRKGA approaches for 2D and 3D bin packing problems use a maximal-space representation to manage free spaces in bins and hybridize a placement procedure with a genetic algorithm based on random keys.\n\n## Benchmark Performance and Gap to Optimal\n\nBRKGA+BLF and BRKGA+OS performed very well, retrieving optimal solutions on 11 and 9 instances, respectively on the 2SPOS test set. BRKGA has been found to be much faster than branch & cut algorithms, with one example showing BRKGA solving instances in 0.0087 cpu seconds versus 1004.00 seconds for the exact algorithm.\n\nThe new approach was extensively tested on 858 problem instances and computational experiment results demonstrate that the new approach consistently equals or outperforms other approaches.\n\n## Overall Effectiveness\n\nBRKGA tends to outperform or match the best approaches in the literature for problems in this category, making it a highly effective metaheuristic for 2D packing benchmarks with minimal gaps to optimal solutions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"11515b51-4573-436a-a3cc-56112c32251a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:50:59.777Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d11069b3-0b2b-4590-9f13-f32d92993e71","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0cddb6b6-c768-464f-87e4-7d7bc56a0479","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FBYzPod4kSc1gjAYYxrYhg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"genetic algorithm\" \"CP-SAT\" warm start mini solve fitness evaluation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"Solving the SAT problem using Genetic Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://www.astesj.com/publications/ASTESJ_020416.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Fitness Functions in Genetic Algorithms: Evaluating Solutions | by Sowmyashri V | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@sowmy3010/fitness-functions-in-genetic-algorithms-evaluating-solutions-1b998f38d6b9\"},{\"title\":\"New Evolutionary Approaches for SAT Solving | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore\",\"url\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8576084\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Solving the 3-SAT problem using genetic algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/287201478_Solving_the_3-SAT_problem_using_genetic_algorithms\"},{\"title\":\"An Improved Adaptive Genetic Algorithm for Solving 3-SAT Problems Based on Effective Restart and Greedy Strategy | Atlantis Press\",\"url\":\"https://www.atlantis-press.com/journals/ijcis/25888772/view\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding your query about \"genetic algorithm,\" \"CP-SAT,\" \"warm start,\" \"mini solve,\" and \"fitness evaluation\":\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Genetic Algorithms and Fitness Evaluation:**\nThe fitness function assigns scores to solutions based on criteria that reflect the objectives of the optimization problem, with criteria carefully chosen to capture desired attributes of an optimal solution. Genetic approaches involve many factors such as crossover, mutation, parent selection and the most important factor, the fitness function.\n\n**CP-SAT Warm Start Capabilities:**\nFirst solution subsolvers are strategies that try to find a first solution as fast as possible and are often used to warm up the solver and to get a first impression of the problem. Additionally, warm-starts are utilized in CP-SAT, where the optimal linear relaxation may be computed deeper down the tree.\n\n**CP-SAT as a Portfolio Solver:**\nCP-SAT is a portfolio-solver that uses different techniques to solve problems with some information exchange between different workers, leading to some redundancy in the search but increasing the chance of running the right technique.\n\nThe search results focus on individual components but don't appear to contain specific literature on combining genetic algorithms with CP-SAT for warm-start initialization or mini-solve fitness evaluation strategies. The results suggest this may be a specialized research area not well-documented in the readily available sources.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0a0b090f-7c5c-499a-801d-a19a6c482d0e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:00.910Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0cddb6b6-c768-464f-87e4-7d7bc56a0479","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b6ee140c-27fc-45db-9072-6ae398a36812","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DRM7LT5MXX2bqYBtdbnhjW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RINS relaxation induced neighborhood vs genetic algorithm MIP warm start\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MIP solutions | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-004-0518-7\"},{\"title\":\"Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MIP solutions | Mathematical Programming: Series A and B\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/s10107-004-0518-7\"},{\"title\":\"A pre-processing aware RINS based MIP heuristic\",\"url\":\"http://www.decom.ufop.br/haroldo/papers/Gomes2013.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Genetic Algorithm Based on Relaxation Induced Neighborhood Search in a Local Branching Framework for Capacitated Multicommodity Network Design | Networks and Spatial Economics\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11067-015-9284-8\"},{\"title\":\"Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MILP solutions | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/200035270_Exploring_relaxation_induced_neighborhoods_to_improve_MILP_solutions\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Exploring relaxation induced neighborhoods to improve MIP solutions | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Exploring-relaxation-induced-neighborhoods-to-MIP-Danna-Rothberg/74e86f325ddfb08f2f79b488ebfac2a43fd5e529\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) RENS-relaxation enforced neighborhood search\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228715008_RENS-relaxation_enforced_neighborhood_search\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A Pre-processing Aware RINS Based MIP Heuristic\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/264557836_A_Pre-processing_Aware_RINS_Based_MIP_Heuristic\"},{\"title\":\"A Pre-processing Aware RINS Based MIP Heuristic | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-38516-2_1\"},{\"title\":\"Relaxation Induced Neighborhood Search (RINS)\",\"url\":\"https://www-eio.upc.edu/lceio/manuals/cplex90/relnotescplex/relnotescplex11.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about RINS, relaxation-induced neighborhoods, genetic algorithms, and MIP warm starts:\n\n## RINS (Relaxation Induced Neighborhood Search)\n\nRINS constructs a promising neighborhood using information contained in the continuous relaxation of the MIP model. RINS sets all the variables that are equal in both solutions - specifically, comparing the incumbent feasible solution with the LP relaxation solution.\n\n## How RINS Works\n\nRINS is a heuristic that explores a neighborhood of the current incumbent to try to find a new, improved incumbent. It formulates the neighborhood exploration as a MIP, a subproblem known as the subMIP, and truncates the subMIP solution by limiting the number of nodes explored in the search tree.\n\n## Genetic Algorithms vs. RINS\n\nThe advantage of RINS over other approaches (e.g., Genetic Algorithms or Simulated Annealing) is that we do not sequentially go through a pool of solutions, but can search in an exponentially-sized space of improvements for the best solution, as they are encoded implicitly by the repair problem.\n\n## Hybrid Approaches\n\nThe search results also show that researchers have combined RINS with genetic algorithms. A GA algorithm is started by initial population which is made by two parents obtained from hybrid LB and RINS algorithms. This represents a warm start technique where genetic algorithms are initialized using solutions from RINS/Local Branching methods.\n\nOther MIP heuristics include local branching, relaxation-induced neighborhood search (RINS), distance-induced neighborhood search (DINS), preprocessing aware RINS (p-RINS), and Solution Polishing, which demonstrates the broader ecosystem of neighborhood search methods used to improve MIP solutions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5e20edb7-c77e-4b4e-ac1e-ca3fdb6d470a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:02.128Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b6ee140c-27fc-45db-9072-6ae398a36812","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"5c6c34a3-901f-4dab-9fc0-056ed56633f0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a0d2d950c3bbd8398","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019UWR7qGLJtTByxpSeKdMgd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"deep reinforcement learning operator selection genetic algorithm packing 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Reinforcement Learning Assisted Autonomous Selection of Sparsity-Aware Genetic Operators for Sparse Large-Scale Multi-Objective Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.26599/TST.2025.9010012\"},{\"title\":\"Constrained Multi-Objective Optimization With Deep Reinforcement Learning Assisted Operator Selection\",\"url\":\"https://www.ieee-jas.net/article/doi/10.1109/JAS.2023.123687\"},{\"title\":\"Optimizing 2D Packing Strategies for Autoclave Loading Using Deep Reinforcement Learning | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-05179-0_4\"},{\"title\":\"Deep Learning-Based Operators for Evolutionary Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2407.10477v1\"},{\"title\":\"Controlling genetic algorithms with reinforcement learning | Proceedings of the 4th Annual Conference on Genetic and Evolutionary Computation\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/2955491.2955607\"},{\"title\":\"adaptive search strategy combination algorithm based on reinforcement learning and neighborhood search | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/177/7984516\"},{\"title\":\"A deep reinforcement learning assisted adaptive genetic algorithm for flexible job shop scheduling - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0952197625004476\"},{\"title\":\"Deep Reinforcement Learning Based Adaptive Operator Selection for Evolutionary Multi-Objective Optimization | IEEE Journals & Magazine | IEEE Xplore\",\"url\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9712324/\"},{\"title\":\"RL-GA: A Reinforcement Learning-based Genetic Algorithm for Electromagnetic Detection Satellite Scheduling Problem - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S221065022300010X\"},{\"title\":\"Selection of Deep Reinforcement Learning Using a Genetic Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://ceur-ws.org/Vol-3171/paper83.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here are the key findings related to your query on deep reinforcement learning, operator selection, genetic algorithms, and packing in 2025:\n\n## Recent Research Highlights\n\n### Reinforcement Learning-Assisted Operator Selection\n\nA reinforcement learning assisted autonomous sparse multi-objective evolutionary algorithm aims to effectively utilize sparse knowledge for designing diversified genetic operators and automatically select appropriate genetic operators for various problems or different situations within the same optimization process.\n\nAn online operator selection framework assisted by Deep Reinforcement Learning treats the dynamics of the population (including convergence, diversity, and feasibility) as the state, candidate operators as actions, and improvement of the population state as the reward.\n\n### Deep Learning-Based Genetic Operators\n\nUnlike conventional GA crossover operators that rely on random selection of parental genes, a deep neural crossover leverages deep reinforcement learning and an encoder-decoder architecture to select genes, using DRL to learn a policy for selecting promising genes.\n\n### Packing Applications\n\nDeep reinforcement learning, specifically the proximal policy optimization (PPO) algorithm, has been investigated to learn effective packing strategies for optimizing autoclave loading. When evaluated against a genetic algorithm across scenarios with regular items, PPO achieved a high fill rate of 99.37%, slightly surpassing GA's 97.50%.\n\n### Flexible Job Shop Scheduling\n\nDeep reinforcement learning is employed to adaptively tune key parameters of GA and dynamically select appropriate genetic operations in recent 2025 research on flexible job shop scheduling problems.\n\nThese advances show growing integration of DRL with genetic algorithms for dynamic operator selection across various optimization domains.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5d6ed73c-768c-4683-bedd-4f1b911bddf7","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:51:03.653Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5c6c34a3-901f-4dab-9fc0-056ed56633f0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
